<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chicama Valley</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chicama Valley is a river valley on the desert north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, just north of Trujillo. Watered by irrigation since ancient times, it was a heartland of the Moche culture, whose major site of El Brujo stands near its mouth, and later of the Chimú, who linked it to the Moche valley with a long canal. The early site of Huaca Prieta lies there, and the valley is now a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>-producing area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chicama-valley.jpg" alt="Chicama Valley" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aparofthere · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ValleDeMoche.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
